WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.830
Looking at the aftermath

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.040
of a wildfire might make you think

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.730
that no good can come from it.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.100
After all, it demolishes homes,

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.900
displaces thousands of people and animals,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.830
and incinerates millions of acres of land.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.760
But, believe it or not,
there are more benefits

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.840
to wildfires than meets the eye.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:27.500
For example, some wildlife
don't just survive the flames;

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.833
they actually thrive in the fire's wake.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.200
In 2001, wildfires in New Mexico destroyed

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.440
the endangered Jemez Mountains
salamander's habitat.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.000
But the population survived by hiding out

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.840
in nooks and crannies of rocks.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.650
It turns out many amphibians
and smaller animals

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:54.000
who can't outrun the
flames take cover in logs,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.520
under rocks, or by burying
themselves in the dirt.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.900
At the other end of the
spectrum, large animals

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.420
like elk and deer flee to
streams or lakes for protection.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.840
Other animals aren't so lucky.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.840
Koalas, for example, will
instinctively crawl up trees,

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.700
trapping themselves in the flames.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.810
But wildfires aren't just a threat;

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.640
they're also an opportunity,
especially for predators

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.100
like bears, raccoons, and raptors

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.690
who hunt down creatures trying to escape.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:30.030
In fact, it's even been reported

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.300
that raptors will
intentionally spread fires

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.743
to flush out prey.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.240
However, the real benefits of fires come

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.280
after the flames die down.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.340
Pine trees, for example,
open up their waxy cones

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.150
and release their seeds during the fire.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:49.870
But it's the ashes the fire leaves behind

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:53.053
that fertilize the soil
so the seeds can grow.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.630
Moreover, the fire removes debris as well

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:00.060
as dead and diseased plants,
which exposes the ground

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.400
to more sunlight in the process.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.020
This helps seedlings
sprout and can sometimes

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.660
even spawn thousands of
flowers in the fire's wake.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.980
And many animals also take advantage

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.510
of a freshly-burned forest.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:17.360
The charred remains of trees
provide the perfect habitat

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.810
for insects and small wildlife.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:23.470
Wood wasps, for instance,
depend on the burned trees

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.100
to lay their eggs.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.010
And other animals like
black-backed woodpeckers

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:32.450
will actively seek out burned
forests for a tasty snack.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.540
They binge on bark beetles
that live in the dead trees

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.800
and make their homes
in the blackened bark.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.510
And while this delicate
balance between destruction

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:45.240
and new growth has been a
way of life for millennia,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.990
human interference is changing that.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:51.500
Over the last century,
we stifled wildfires

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:55.290
so forests became unnaturally overgrown

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.280
and contained plenty of
deadwood just waiting

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.040
to ignite like a ticking time bomb.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.440
This, along with increased temperatures

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.220
and more droughts, has made
wildfires more devastating

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.730
and frequent than ever before
in places like California,

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.800
making it harder for plants and animals

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.170
to rebound like they used to.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.760
So if we don't start
clearing dead vegetation

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.860
and helping fires run
their natural course,

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.930
we may end up threatening
forests and wildlife

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.430
more than any fire ever could.

